ething that is  obviously going to get serious  consideration i mean i think  i think the the fact that it looks like  these these remarkable men have done  great brave deeds escape the saying he's  not denied i think the  the thing that is really coming across  and is very striking and very concerning  is that there seemed to be a remarkable  lack of a plan and this was all being  done very last minute we would know for  18 months that the americans were going  to withdraw  and whilst nobody denies that the  taliban has taken over considerably  quicker than anybody predicted including  themselves nevertheless uh it is  striking that there seems to have been  and it's very it is wrong and it's the  fault of this government there hasn't  been a proper plan to evacuate everybody  that should have been evacuated and also  make plans for the future of so many of  these people we still have no details  for example of what afghan refugees  coming to our country where they're  going to live what  the actual arrangements uh for people to  even begin to try to apply to be refuge  refugees to come over to the uk and  there's no plan on any of this it really  is shocking uh meanwhile kate the ft uh  looking at the continued terror threat  and the fact that it is still a very  volatile situation with with fresh  attacks being foiled  yes and it's confirming that the u.s  launched a drone strike um to try to  offset another terror attack on the  kabul airport i saw abc just moments ago  was reporting that  six people died several of them children  um from having to thwart that attack but  there was a car that reportedly was  holding uh lots of bombs it was thought  to be another uh suicide bombing attempt  uh which was stopped by the us but you  know this is raising a lot of questions  the u.s is still there they're still  pushing forward their evacuation efforts  there's still concerns that there could  be yet another attack they're dealing  with isis k the the regional uh group  for isis there which is extremely  violent and pressing forward still with  its attempts to squander the evacuation  and you have jake sullivan the u.s  national security adviser who has said  and i believe quote the president does  not intend to start a new war in  afghanistan but there is now there are  now plans and the president has pledged  that he's going to go after those uh who  set off that first bomb and that there  is probably going to be an increased use  of drones so the idea that the us is out  of afghanistan completely and for good  simply isn't the case it's going to be  more of a matter of boots on the ground  and i i don't think we're necessarily  looking at the end of the u.s presence  in afghanistan but a different kind of  warfare which obviously raises a lot  more questions is this the the end of  the beginning of the of the evacuation  and the retreat and and as anna  mentioned before um unfortunately for  the brits and for almost certainly for  the americans as well they're going to  be many people left behind you should  have gotten out what happens to those  people indeed uh the guardian looking at  um the fact that the taliban saying that  they'll allow more departures  from the airport um it's a forced  corporation isn't it that the u.s are  going to have to have with the taliban  in terms of the the continued evacuation  anna  yes i mean if it enables them to get  some of the people out that they have to  get out there will still be people  there that haven't  been um taken out of afghanistan  including the people who supported the  british  armed forces ngos and so on and so forth  and of course we know that there are  huge pressures on the border i'm sorry  i'd i wouldn't trust the taliban further  than i could throw them  so uh the idea i find it quite  incredible that the americans are going  to somehow stay and  have a presence in afghanistan by  working with the taliban as quite  extraordinary i mean you have to hear  the report