er departments and of course  they've got this huge backlog which  they're trying to deal with but i think  that the the short thing is is that  actually we have a crisis that is  developing very quickly in our gp  practices  and in the meantime they've got to open  up because if you like the pr  is not good and there are a lot of  people who believe you can't get to see  your gp in any event  and kate the guardian uh suggesting that  an estimated eight million people in  england with mental health problems  can't get specialist help  so getting access to mental health care  was a really big problem on the nhs  before covet hit and it was very  difficult in many areas to be able to to  see a therapist or a psychiatrist um if  you needed that kind of care for your  anxiety depression and other mental  health illnesses cobit has made the  problem a lot worse it's also grown the  number of people very understandably who  want to get access to these services and  being locked up being in lock down for  months on end is not a normal human  condition and a lot of people have  suffered the mental consequences of that  is yet another example of this huge  backlog we have now in the nhs it's  estimated to be over 5 million on nhs  england now but the health secretary  estimates it could go up to 13 million  people those sound like big numbers but  i don't think we have acutely felt what  that will actually mean if people aren't  getting access to these kinds of key  treatments you know yet another reminder  that mental health should be taken as  seriously as physical health but that we  were woeful on this before the pandemic  hit and now i think we're really going  to start to feel the consequences of  that

